{"name":"Melrose","hex":"#c3b9dd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Melrose","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A small town in the Scottish Borders council area, Scotland .","A small town in South Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Melrose"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melrose, Scottish Borders","blocks":[{"text":"Melrose is a historic market town and civil parish in the Scottish Borders, Scotland. Historically part of the historic county of Roxburghshire, it lies near the River Tweed, approximately 30 miles southeast of Edinburgh, 62 miles east-southeast of Glasgow, 63 miles southeast of Perth, and 3 miles west-northwest of Galashiels. It had a population of 2,473 in 2022."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Melrose_Market_Square_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4050626.jpg/330px-Melrose_Market_Square_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4050626.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melrose%2C_Scottish_Borders","wikidata":"Q632993"},{"title":"Melrose Abbey","blocks":[{"text":"St Mary's Abbey, Melrose is a partly ruined monastery of the Cistercian order in Melrose, Roxburghshire, in the Scottish Borders. It was founded in 1136 by Cistercian monks at the request of King David I of Scotland and was the chief house of that order in the country until the Reformation. It was headed by the abbot or commendator of Melrose. Today the abbey is maintained by Historic Environment Scotland as a scheduled monument."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6a/Ruins_of_St_Mary%27s_Abbey%2C_Melrose_%28Scotland%27s_first_Cistercian_monastery%2C_1136%29%2C_showing_late_14th-century_Gothic_architecture_in_rose-colored_sandstone_with_medieval_cemetery%2C_burial_place_of_Robert_the_Bruce%27s_heart%2C_Scottish_Borders.jpg/330px-thumbnail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.w